#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531792 :question/problem "A surface $S$ is defined by $z=f(x,y).$ The curve $C$ is the intersection of $S$ with the plane $x=-2.$ Given that the point $P(-2,-3,-3)$ lies on $C,$ and that $f_y = \\dfrac{4x}y - 4x^2y$, find the slope of the tangent to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=-2.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P(-2,-3,-3)$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(-2,-3).$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(-2,-3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(-2,-3) & =\\frac{4(-2)}{(-3)}-4(-2)^2(-3) \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{8}{3}\\right)+(48) \\\\  & =\\frac{152}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531794 :question/problem "A surface $S$ is defined by $z=f(x,y).$ The curve $C$ is the intersection of $S$ with the plane $x=3.$ Given that the point $P(3,4,5)$ lies on $C,$ and that $f_y = \\dfrac{5x}y - 5x^2y+4\\cos(\\pi y)$, find the slope of the tangent to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=3.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P(3,4,5)$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(3,4).$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(3,4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(3,4) & =\\frac{5(3)}{(4)}-5(3)^2(4)+4\\cos(\\pi (4)) \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{15}{4}\\right)+(-180)+(4) \\\\  & =- \\frac{689}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531800 :question/problem "A surface $S$ is defined by $z=f(x,y).$ The curve $C$ is the intersection of $S$ with the plane $x=-3.$ Given that the point $P(-3,4,4)$ lies on $C,$ and that $f_y = \\dfrac{2x}y - 2x^2y$, find the slope of the tangent to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=-3.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P(-3,4,4)$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(-3,4).$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(-3,4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(-3,4) & =\\frac{2(-3)}{(4)}-2(-3)^2(4) \\\\  & =\\left(- \\frac{3}{2}\\right)+(-72) \\\\  & =- \\frac{147}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531811 :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨z,\\,x,\\,y⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere the parametric surface $S$ is defined by\n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨2 u + v + 1,\\,u + v,\\,u - v⟩\n$$\n\nover the domain $D={(u,v)\\,:\\,-1\\le u\\le 3,\\,-1\\le v\\le 3}.$\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}=⟨-2,\\,3,\\,1⟩.$*" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe're given that the fundamental vector product for our surface is\n\n$$\n{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}=⟨-2,\\,3,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\nF(r(u,v))=⟨u - v,\\,2 u + v + 1,\\,u + v⟩\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the dot product of $F$ and ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n⟨u - v,\\,2 u + v + 1,\\,u + v⟩\\cdot ⟨-2,\\,3,\\,1⟩=5 u + 6 v + 3\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral evaluates to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={\\int }_{-1}^{3}{\\int }_{-1}^{3}(5 u + 6 v + 3)\\,\\text{d}v\\,\\text{d}u \\\\ & ={\\int }_{-1}^{3}[v \\left(5 u + 3 v + 3\\right)]_{v=-1}^{v=3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\ & ={\\int }_{-1}^{3}(20 u + 36)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\ & =[2 u \\left(5 u + 18\\right)]_{-1}^{3} \\\\ & =224.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531812 :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨z,\\,x,\\,y⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere the parametric surface $S$ is defined by\n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨2 u + v + 1,\\,u + v,\\,u - v⟩\n$$\n\nover the domain $D={(u,v)\\,:\\,-2\\le u\\le 1,\\,-1\\le v\\le 3}.$\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}=⟨-2,\\,3,\\,1⟩.$*" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe're given that the fundamental vector product for our surface is\n\n$$\n{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}=⟨-2,\\,3,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\nF(r(u,v))=⟨u - v,\\,2 u + v + 1,\\,u + v⟩\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the dot product of $F$ and ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n⟨u - v,\\,2 u + v + 1,\\,u + v⟩\\cdot ⟨-2,\\,3,\\,1⟩=5 u + 6 v + 3\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral evaluates to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={\\int }_{-2}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{3}(5 u + 6 v + 3)\\,\\text{d}v\\,\\text{d}u \\\\ & ={\\int }_{-2}^{1}[v \\left(5 u + 3 v + 3\\right)]_{v=-1}^{v=3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\ & ={\\int }_{-2}^{1}(20 u + 36)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\ & =[2 u \\left(5 u + 18\\right)]_{-2}^{1} \\\\ & =78.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531818 :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨z,\\,x,\\,y⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere the parametric surface $S$ is defined by\n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨2 u + v + 1,\\,u + v,\\,u - v⟩\n$$\n\nover the domain $D={(u,v)\\,:\\,-3\\le u\\le 1,\\,2\\le v\\le 4}.$\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}=⟨-2,\\,3,\\,1⟩.$*" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe're given that the fundamental vector product for our surface is\n\n$$\n{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}=⟨-2,\\,3,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\nF(r(u,v))=⟨u - v,\\,2 u + v + 1,\\,u + v⟩\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the dot product of $F$ and ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n⟨u - v,\\,2 u + v + 1,\\,u + v⟩\\cdot ⟨-2,\\,3,\\,1⟩=5 u + 6 v + 3\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral evaluates to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={\\int }_{-3}^{1}{\\int }_{2}^{4}(5 u + 6 v + 3)\\,\\text{d}v\\,\\text{d}u \\\\ & ={\\int }_{-3}^{1}[v \\left(5 u + 3 v + 3\\right)]_{v=2}^{v=4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\ & ={\\int }_{-3}^{1}(10 u + 42)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\ & =[u \\left(5 u + 42\\right)]_{-3}^{1} \\\\ & =128.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531826 :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨z,\\,x,\\,y⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere the parametric surface $S$ is defined by\n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨2 u + v + 1,\\,u + v,\\,u - v⟩\n$$\n\nover the domain $D={(u,v)\\,:\\,2\\le u\\le 4,\\,2\\le v\\le 4}.$\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}=⟨-2,\\,3,\\,1⟩.$*" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe're given that the fundamental vector product for our surface is\n\n$$\n{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}=⟨-2,\\,3,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\nF(r(u,v))=⟨u - v,\\,2 u + v + 1,\\,u + v⟩\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the dot product of $F$ and ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n⟨u - v,\\,2 u + v + 1,\\,u + v⟩\\cdot ⟨-2,\\,3,\\,1⟩=5 u + 6 v + 3\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral evaluates to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={\\int }_{2}^{4}{\\int }_{2}^{4}(5 u + 6 v + 3)\\,\\text{d}v\\,\\text{d}u \\\\ & ={\\int }_{2}^{4}[v \\left(5 u + 3 v + 3\\right)]_{v=2}^{v=4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\ & ={\\int }_{2}^{4}(10 u + 42)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\ & =[u \\left(5 u + 42\\right)]_{2}^{4} \\\\ & =144.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531827 :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨z,\\,x,\\,y⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere the parametric surface $S$ is defined by\n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨2 u + v + 1,\\,u + v,\\,u - v⟩\n$$\n\nover the domain $D={(u,v)\\,:\\,-3\\le u\\le 0,\\,1\\le v\\le 2}.$\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}=⟨-2,\\,3,\\,1⟩.$*" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe're given that the fundamental vector product for our surface is\n\n$$\n{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}=⟨-2,\\,3,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\nF(r(u,v))=⟨u - v,\\,2 u + v + 1,\\,u + v⟩\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the dot product of $F$ and ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n⟨u - v,\\,2 u + v + 1,\\,u + v⟩\\cdot ⟨-2,\\,3,\\,1⟩=5 u + 6 v + 3\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral evaluates to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={\\int }_{-3}^{0}{\\int }_{1}^{2}(5 u + 6 v + 3)\\,\\text{d}v\\,\\text{d}u \\\\ & ={\\int }_{-3}^{0}[v \\left(5 u + 3 v + 3\\right)]_{v=1}^{v=2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\ & ={\\int }_{-3}^{0}(5 u + 12)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\ & =[\\frac{u \\left(5 u + 24\\right)}{2}]_{-3}^{0} \\\\ & =\\frac{27}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531860 :question/problem "What is the minimum value of $f(x)=3\\cos(x+90^{\\circ})+2$?" :question/worked-solution "The range of $\\cos x$ is\n\n$$\n-1\\le \\cos x\\le 1.\n$$\n\nHorizontal shifts and stretches have no effect on the range. Therefore,\n\n$$\n-1\\le \\cos(x+90^{\\circ})\\le 1.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the above inequality by $3$ and then adding $2$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & -1\\le \\cos(x+90^{\\circ})\\le 1 \\\\  & -3\\le 3\\cos(x+90^{\\circ})\\le 3 \\\\  & -1\\le 3\\cos(x+90^{\\circ})+2\\le 5 \\\\  & -1\\le f(x)\\le 5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value is $-1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531861 :question/problem "What is the range of the function $$\n\\displaystyle y=- \\frac{7}{3}\\sin(x+90^{\\circ})+\\frac{15}{4}\n$$?" :question/worked-solution "The range of $\\sin x$ is\n\n$$\n-1\\le \\sin x\\le 1.\n$$\n\nHorizontal shifts and stretches have no effect on the range. Therefore,\n\n$$\n-1\\le \\sin(x+90^{\\circ})\\le 1.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the above inequality by $- \\frac{7}{3}$ and then adding $\\frac{15}{4}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & -1\\le \\sin(x+90^{\\circ})\\le 1 \\\\  & \\frac{7}{3}\\ge - \\frac{7}{3}\\sin(x+90^{\\circ})\\ge - \\frac{7}{3} \\\\  & \\frac{73}{12}\\ge - \\frac{7}{3}\\sin(x+90^{\\circ})+\\frac{15}{4}\\ge \\frac{17}{12} \\\\  & \\frac{17}{12}\\le y\\le \\frac{73}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the range is $y\\in [\\frac{17}{12},\\frac{73}{12}].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531878 :question/problem "What is the minimum value of $f(x)=-3\\sin(2x+1)+2$?" :question/worked-solution "The range of $\\sin x$ is\n\n$$\n-1\\le \\sin x\\le 1.\n$$\n\nHorizontal shifts and stretches have no effect on the range. Therefore,\n\n$$\n-1\\le \\sin(2x+1)\\le 1.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the above inequality by $-3$ and then adding $2$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & -1\\le \\sin(2x+1)\\le 1 \\\\  & 3\\ge -3\\sin(2x+1)\\ge -3 \\\\  & 5\\ge -3\\sin(2x+1)+2\\ge -1 \\\\  & -1\\le f(x)\\le 5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value is $-1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531882 :question/problem "What is the range of the function $$\n\\displaystyle y=- \\frac{9}{2}\\sin(3x+\\pi)-\\frac{10}{3}\n$$?" :question/worked-solution "The range of $\\sin x$ is\n\n$$\n-1\\le \\sin x\\le 1.\n$$\n\nHorizontal shifts and stretches have no effect on the range. Therefore,\n\n$$\n-1\\le \\sin(3x+\\pi)\\le 1.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the above inequality by $- \\frac{9}{2}$ and then subtracting $\\frac{10}{3}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & -1\\le \\sin(3x+\\pi)\\le 1 \\\\  & \\frac{9}{2}\\ge - \\frac{9}{2}\\sin(3x+\\pi)\\ge - \\frac{9}{2} \\\\  & \\frac{7}{6}\\ge - \\frac{9}{2}\\sin(3x+\\pi)-\\frac{10}{3}\\ge - \\frac{47}{6} \\\\  & - \\frac{47}{6}\\le y\\le \\frac{7}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the range is $y\\in [- \\frac{47}{6},\\frac{7}{6}].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531883 :question/problem "What is the minimum value of $f(x)=-6\\sin(4x-3)+8$?" :question/worked-solution "The range of $\\sin x$ is\n\n$$\n-1\\le \\sin x\\le 1.\n$$\n\nHorizontal shifts and stretches have no effect on the range. Therefore,\n\n$$\n-1\\le \\sin(4x-3)\\le 1.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the above inequality by $-6$ and then adding $8$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & -1\\le \\sin(4x-3)\\le 1 \\\\  & 6\\ge -6\\sin(4x-3)\\ge -6 \\\\  & 14\\ge -6\\sin(4x-3)+8\\ge 2 \\\\  & 2\\le f(x)\\le 14.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value is $2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531895 :question/problem "What is the range of the function $$\n\\displaystyle y=- \\frac{5}{2}\\cos(x-15^{\\circ})-\\frac{14}{3}\n$$?" :question/worked-solution "The range of $\\cos x$ is\n\n$$\n-1\\le \\cos x\\le 1.\n$$\n\nHorizontal shifts and stretches have no effect on the range. Therefore,\n\n$$\n-1\\le \\cos(x-15^{\\circ})\\le 1.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the above inequality by $- \\frac{5}{2}$ and then subtracting $\\frac{14}{3}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & -1\\le \\cos(x-15^{\\circ})\\le 1 \\\\  & \\frac{5}{2}\\ge - \\frac{5}{2}\\cos(x-15^{\\circ})\\ge - \\frac{5}{2} \\\\  & - \\frac{13}{6}\\ge - \\frac{5}{2}\\cos(x-15^{\\circ})-\\frac{14}{3}\\ge - \\frac{43}{6} \\\\  & - \\frac{43}{6}\\le y\\le - \\frac{13}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the range is $y\\in [- \\frac{43}{6},- \\frac{13}{6}].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531896 :question/problem "What is the minimum value of $f(x)=-\\cos(4x+2)+4$?" :question/worked-solution "The range of $\\cos x$ is\n\n$$\n-1\\le \\cos x\\le 1.\n$$\n\nHorizontal shifts and stretches have no effect on the range. Therefore,\n\n$$\n-1\\le \\cos(4x+2)\\le 1.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the above inequality by $-1$ and then adding $4$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & -1\\le \\cos(4x+2)\\le 1 \\\\  & 1\\ge -\\cos(4x+2)\\ge -1 \\\\  & 5\\ge -\\cos(4x+2)+4\\ge 3 \\\\  & 3\\le f(x)\\le 5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value is $3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531909 :question/problem "What is the range of the function $$\n\\displaystyle y=- \\frac{4}{3}\\sin(4x+\\frac{\\pi}{6})+\\frac{14}{3}\n$$?" :question/worked-solution "The range of $\\sin x$ is\n\n$$\n-1\\le \\sin x\\le 1.\n$$\n\nHorizontal shifts and stretches have no effect on the range. Therefore,\n\n$$\n-1\\le \\sin(4x+\\frac{\\pi}{6})\\le 1.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the above inequality by $- \\frac{4}{3}$ and then adding $\\frac{14}{3}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & -1\\le \\sin(4x+\\frac{\\pi}{6})\\le 1 \\\\  & \\frac{4}{3}\\ge - \\frac{4}{3}\\sin(4x+\\frac{\\pi}{6})\\ge - \\frac{4}{3} \\\\  & 6\\ge - \\frac{4}{3}\\sin(4x+\\frac{\\pi}{6})+\\frac{14}{3}\\ge \\frac{10}{3} \\\\  & \\frac{10}{3}\\le y\\le 6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the range is $y\\in [\\frac{10}{3},6].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531910 :question/problem "What is the range of the function $y=4\\cos(2x-3)-6$?" :question/worked-solution "The range of $\\cos x$ is\n\n$$\n-1\\le \\cos x\\le 1.\n$$\n\nHorizontal shifts and stretches have no effect on the range. Therefore,\n\n$$\n-1\\le \\cos(2x-3)\\le 1.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the above inequality by $4$ and then subtracting $6$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & -1\\le \\cos(2x-3)\\le 1 \\\\  & -4\\le 4\\cos(2x-3)\\le 4 \\\\  & -10\\le 4\\cos(2x-3)-6\\le -2 \\\\  & -10\\le y\\le -2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the range is $y\\in [-10,-2].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531915 :question/problem "What is the minimum value of $$\n\\displaystyle f(x)=- \\frac{5}{2}\\cos(5x-2)-\\frac{11}{2}\n$$?" :question/worked-solution "The range of $\\cos x$ is\n\n$$\n-1\\le \\cos x\\le 1.\n$$\n\nHorizontal shifts and stretches have no effect on the range. Therefore,\n\n$$\n-1\\le \\cos(5x-2)\\le 1.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the above inequality by $- \\frac{5}{2}$ and then subtracting $\\frac{11}{2}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & -1\\le \\cos(5x-2)\\le 1 \\\\  & \\frac{5}{2}\\ge - \\frac{5}{2}\\cos(5x-2)\\ge - \\frac{5}{2} \\\\  & -3\\ge - \\frac{5}{2}\\cos(5x-2)-\\frac{11}{2}\\ge -8 \\\\  & -8\\le f(x)\\le -3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value is $-8.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532677 :question/problem "Given that $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)=x{e}^{y}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+y{x}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j},$ what is the scalar curl of $\\mathbf{F}?$" :question/worked-solution "If $\\mathbf{F}=P\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}$ is a vector field on ${\\mathbb{R}}^{2}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $\\mathbf{F}$ all exist, then the *scalar* curl of $\\mathbf{F}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y{x}^{2})-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{e}^{y}) \\\\  & =2xy-x{e}^{y} \\\\  & =x(2y-{e}^{y}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532776 :question/problem "![](images/ac/ac756d7cbd687a23a5b2c1ffcb368298f4676178e8bebccbadd4e837fe66a3d7.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z),$ shown above. Given that $F(x,y,z)$ is independent of $z,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{curl}\\,F$ denotes the curl of the vector field $F.$\n\nTo understand intuitively whether a vector field has curl at a point $P$, imagine that $F$ represents the velocity field of flowing water. Place a paddlewheel at $P$ with its axis in the $z$-direction. If it rotates, the field has nonzero curl; if it does not, the field has zero curl.\n\nThe arrows turn a paddlewheel counterclockwise. Thus $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0$ throughout space, so $F$ is not irrotational. Curl measures local rotation, even when the arrows also spread outward. Wrap the fingers of your right hand in the counterclockwise direction: your thumb points out of the screen, in the positive $z$-direction.\n\nWith that in mind, check each statement:\n\n- Statement $\\text{curl}\\,F=0$ is false.\n- Statement $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0$ is true.\n- Statement “$F$ is irrotational” is false.\n- Statement “$\\text{curl}\\,F$ points in the positive $z$-direction” is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\text{curl}\\,F\\ne 0$; “$\\text{curl}\\,F$ points in the positive $z$-direction” only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532783 :question/problem "Calculate the period of the function $$\n\\displaystyle f(x)=-6\\sin\\left(-\\frac{7}{8}\\left(4x+\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\right)\\right).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a function in the form $y=A\\sin(Bx+C)+D,$ the period is $\\frac{2\\pi }{|B|}.$\n\nThe coefficient of $x$ in the argument is $$\n\\displaystyle B=\\left(-\\frac{7}{8}\\right)(4)=-\\frac{7}{2}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have that $B=-\\frac{7}{2}.$ Therefore, the period is $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{2\\pi }{|-\\frac{7}{2}|}=\\frac{2\\pi }{\\frac{7}{2}}=\\frac{4\\pi }{7}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532784 :question/problem "Calculate the period of the function $$\n\\displaystyle f(x)=-6\\cos\\left(-\\frac{7}{5}x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a function in the form $y=A\\cos(Bx+C)+D,$ the period is $\\frac{2\\pi }{|B|}.$\n\nIn our case, we have that $B=-\\frac{7}{5}.$ Therefore, the period is $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{2\\pi }{|-\\frac{7}{5}|}=\\frac{2\\pi }{\\frac{7}{5}}=\\frac{10\\pi }{7}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532792 :question/problem "Calculate the period of the function $$\n\\displaystyle f(x)=-4\\cos\\left(-\\frac{2}{5}x+\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a function in the form $y=A\\cos(Bx+C)+D,$ the period is $\\frac{2\\pi }{|B|}.$\n\nIn our case, we have that $B=-\\frac{2}{5}.$ Therefore, the period is $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{2\\pi }{|-\\frac{2}{5}|}=\\frac{2\\pi }{\\frac{2}{5}}=5\\pi .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532798 :question/problem "Calculate the period of the function $$\n\\displaystyle f(x)=6\\sin\\left(-\\frac{1}{4}x-\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a function in the form $y=A\\sin(Bx+C)+D,$ the period is $\\frac{2\\pi }{|B|}.$\n\nIn our case, we have that $B=-\\frac{1}{4}.$ Therefore, the period is $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{2\\pi }{|-\\frac{1}{4}|}=\\frac{2\\pi }{\\frac{1}{4}}=8\\pi .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532803 :question/problem "Calculate the period of the function $$\n\\displaystyle f(x)=6\\sin\\left(-\\frac{1}{6}\\left(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)\\right).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a function in the form $y=A\\sin(Bx+C)+D,$ the period is $\\frac{2\\pi }{|B|}.$\n\nThe coefficient of $x$ in the argument is $$\n\\displaystyle B=\\left(-\\frac{1}{6}\\right)(2)=-\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have that $B=-\\frac{1}{3}.$ Therefore, the period is $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{2\\pi }{|-\\frac{1}{3}|}=\\frac{2\\pi }{\\frac{1}{3}}=6\\pi .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532817 :question/problem "Calculate the period of the function $$\n\\displaystyle f(x)=2\\cos\\left(-\\frac{11}{10}\\left(4x-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)\\right).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a function in the form $y=A\\cos(Bx+C)+D,$ the period is $\\frac{2\\pi }{|B|}.$\n\nThe coefficient of $x$ in the argument is $$\n\\displaystyle B=\\left(-\\frac{11}{10}\\right)(4)=-\\frac{22}{5}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have that $B=-\\frac{22}{5}.$ Therefore, the period is $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{2\\pi }{|-\\frac{22}{5}|}=\\frac{2\\pi }{\\frac{22}{5}}=\\frac{5\\pi }{11}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532819 :question/problem "Calculate the period of the function $$\n\\displaystyle f(x)=-6\\sin\\left(-\\frac{9}{5}x+\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\right).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a function in the form $y=A\\sin(Bx+C)+D,$ the period is $\\frac{2\\pi }{|B|}.$\n\nIn our case, we have that $B=-\\frac{9}{5}.$ Therefore, the period is $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{2\\pi }{|-\\frac{9}{5}|}=\\frac{2\\pi }{\\frac{9}{5}}=\\frac{10\\pi }{9}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532825 :question/problem "Calculate the period of the function $$\n\\displaystyle f(x)=-6\\sin\\left(-\\frac{11}{10}\\left(5x-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)\\right).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a function in the form $y=A\\sin(Bx+C)+D,$ the period is $\\frac{2\\pi }{|B|}.$\n\nThe coefficient of $x$ in the argument is $$\n\\displaystyle B=\\left(-\\frac{11}{10}\\right)(5)=-\\frac{11}{2}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have that $B=-\\frac{11}{2}.$ Therefore, the period is $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{2\\pi }{|-\\frac{11}{2}|}=\\frac{2\\pi }{\\frac{11}{2}}=\\frac{4\\pi }{11}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532829 :question/problem "Calculate the period of the function $y = \\frac{1}{5}\\cos (\\frac{1}{4}x)$." :question/worked-solution "Given a function in the form $y=A\\cos(Bx+C)+D,$ the period is $\\frac{2\\pi }{|B|}.$\n\nIn our case, we have that $B=\\frac{1}{4}.$ Therefore, the period is $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{2\\pi }{|\\frac{1}{4}|}=\\frac{2\\pi }{\\frac{1}{4}}=8\\pi .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532831 :question/problem "Calculate the period of the function $$\n\\displaystyle f(x)=-4\\cos\\left(-\\frac{1}{10}\\left(4x+\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)\\right).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a function in the form $y=A\\cos(Bx+C)+D,$ the period is $\\frac{2\\pi }{|B|}.$\n\nThe coefficient of $x$ in the argument is $$\n\\displaystyle B=\\left(-\\frac{1}{10}\\right)(4)=-\\frac{2}{5}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have that $B=-\\frac{2}{5}.$ Therefore, the period is $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{2\\pi }{|-\\frac{2}{5}|}=\\frac{2\\pi }{\\frac{2}{5}}=5\\pi .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532843 :question/problem "Calculate the period of the function $$\n\\displaystyle f(x)=-3\\cos\\left(-\\frac{1}{6}x-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a function in the form $y=A\\cos(Bx+C)+D,$ the period is $\\frac{2\\pi }{|B|}.$\n\nIn our case, we have that $B=-\\frac{1}{6}.$ Therefore, the period is $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{2\\pi }{|-\\frac{1}{6}|}=\\frac{2\\pi }{\\frac{1}{6}}=12\\pi .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532864 :question/problem "Find the derivative of $3f(t)+3g(t)$ with respect to $t$, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t)&=(t^{3} + 2)\\,i- \\frac{1}{t + 2}\\,j+\\cos{\\left(3 t \\right)}\\,k \\\\ g(t)&=(2 t - 1)\\,i+e^{4 t}\\,j+\\cos{\\left(3 t \\right)}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the constant multiple rule and the sum rule, we have\n\n$$\n(3f(t)+3g(t))'=3f'(\\!t)+3g'(\\!t).\n$$\n\nLet's find the derivatives of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f'(t)&=⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t^{3} + 2),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(- \\frac{1}{t + 2}\\right),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\cos{\\left(3 t \\right)})⟩ \\\\ &=3 t^{2}\\,i+\\frac{1}{\\left(t + 2\\right)^{2}}\\,j- 3 \\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)}\\,k \\\\ g'(t)&=⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2 t - 1),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(e^{4 t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\cos{\\left(3 t \\right)})⟩ \\\\ &=2\\,i+4 e^{4 t}\\,j- 3 \\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3f(t)+3g(t))'&=3f'(\\!t)+3g'(\\!t) \\\\ &=3\\left(3 t^{2}\\,i+\\frac{1}{\\left(t + 2\\right)^{2}}\\,j- 3 \\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)}\\,k\\right)+3\\left(2\\,i+4 e^{4 t}\\,j- 3 \\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)}\\,k\\right) \\\\ &=\\left(9 t^{2}\\,i+\\frac{3}{\\left(t + 2\\right)^{2}}\\,j- 9 \\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)}\\,k\\right)+\\left(6\\,i+12 e^{4 t}\\,j- 9 \\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)}\\,k\\right) \\\\ &=(9 t^{2} + 6)\\,i+(12 e^{4 t} + \\frac{3}{\\left(t + 2\\right)^{2}})\\,j- 18 \\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)}\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532870 :question/problem "Find the derivative of $-2f(t)+2g(t)$ with respect to $t$, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t)&=(4 t^{2} + 1)\\,i- \\frac{4}{t - 3}\\,j+\\cos{\\left(3 t \\right)}\\,k \\\\ g(t)&=(t - 4)\\,i+e^{4 t}\\,j+\\cos{\\left(3 t \\right)}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the constant multiple rule and the sum rule, we have\n\n$$\n(-2f(t)+2g(t))'=-2f'(\\!t)+2g'(\\!t).\n$$\n\nLet's find the derivatives of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f'(t)&=⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4 t^{2} + 1),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(- \\frac{4}{t - 3}\\right),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\cos{\\left(3 t \\right)})⟩ \\\\ &=8 t\\,i+\\frac{4}{\\left(t - 3\\right)^{2}}\\,j- 3 \\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)}\\,k \\\\ g'(t)&=⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t - 4),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(e^{4 t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\cos{\\left(3 t \\right)})⟩ \\\\ &=1\\,i+4 e^{4 t}\\,j- 3 \\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-2f(t)+2g(t))'&=-2f'(\\!t)+2g'(\\!t) \\\\ &=-2\\left(8 t\\,i+\\frac{4}{\\left(t - 3\\right)^{2}}\\,j- 3 \\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)}\\,k\\right)+2\\left(1\\,i+4 e^{4 t}\\,j- 3 \\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)}\\,k\\right) \\\\ &=\\left(- 16 t\\,i- \\frac{8}{\\left(t - 3\\right)^{2}}\\,j+6 \\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)}\\,k\\right)+\\left(2\\,i+8 e^{4 t}\\,j- 6 \\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)}\\,k\\right) \\\\ &=(2 - 16 t)\\,i+(8 e^{4 t} - \\frac{8}{\\left(t - 3\\right)^{2}})\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532876 :question/problem "Find the value of $(3h(t) - 2g(t))^{′}$ at $t = 2$, if\n$$\n\\displaystyle h(t)= ⟨6 t^{2},\\,3 - t,\\,3 t^{3} + 24⟩; g(t)= ⟨3 t \\left(t + 2\\right),\\,-8,\\,6 t^{3}⟩\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the constant multiple rule and the sum rule, we have\n\n$$\n(3h(t)-2g(t))'=3h'(\\!t)-2g'(\\!t).\n$$\n\nLet's find the derivatives of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h'(t)&=⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6 t^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3 - t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3 t^{3} + 24)⟩ \\\\ &=⟨12 t,\\,-1,\\,9 t^{2}⟩ \\\\ g'(t)&=⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3 t \\left(t + 2\\right)),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-8),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6 t^{3})⟩ \\\\ &=⟨6 t + 6,\\,0,\\,18 t^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3h(t)-2g(t))'\\big|_{t=2}&=3h'(\\!2)-2g'(\\!2) \\\\ &=3\\left(⟨24,\\,-1,\\,36⟩\\right)-2\\left(⟨18,\\,0,\\,72⟩\\right) \\\\ &=\\left(⟨72,\\,-3,\\,108⟩\\right)+\\left(⟨-36,\\,0,\\,-144⟩\\right) \\\\ &=⟨36,\\,-3,\\,-36⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532877 :question/problem "Find the value of $(3h(t) - 2g(t))^{′}$ at $t = 2$, if\n$$\n\\displaystyle h(t)= ⟨2 t^{2},\\,9 - 3 t,\\,- 2 t^{3} - 16⟩; g(t)= ⟨t \\left(t + 2\\right),\\,-24,\\,- 4 t^{3}⟩\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the constant multiple rule and the sum rule, we have\n\n$$\n(3h(t)-2g(t))'=3h'(\\!t)-2g'(\\!t).\n$$\n\nLet's find the derivatives of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h'(t)&=⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2 t^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(9 - 3 t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(- 2 t^{3} - 16)⟩ \\\\ &=⟨4 t,\\,-3,\\,- 6 t^{2}⟩ \\\\ g'(t)&=⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t \\left(t + 2\\right)),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-24),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(- 4 t^{3})⟩ \\\\ &=⟨2 t + 2,\\,0,\\,- 12 t^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3h(t)-2g(t))'\\big|_{t=2}&=3h'(\\!2)-2g'(\\!2) \\\\ &=3\\left(⟨8,\\,-3,\\,-24⟩\\right)-2\\left(⟨6,\\,0,\\,-48⟩\\right) \\\\ &=\\left(⟨24,\\,-9,\\,-72⟩\\right)+\\left(⟨-12,\\,0,\\,96⟩\\right) \\\\ &=⟨12,\\,-9,\\,24⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532882 :question/problem "Find the derivative of $2f(t)+2g(t)$ with respect to $t$, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t)&=(2 t^{3} + 4)\\,i- \\frac{2}{t - 1}\\,j+\\cos{\\left(4 t \\right)}\\,k \\\\ g(t)&=(4 t - 2)\\,i+e^{4 t}\\,j+\\cos{\\left(4 t \\right)}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the constant multiple rule and the sum rule, we have\n\n$$\n(2f(t)+2g(t))'=2f'(\\!t)+2g'(\\!t).\n$$\n\nLet's find the derivatives of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f'(t)&=⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2 t^{3} + 4),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(- \\frac{2}{t - 1}\\right),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\cos{\\left(4 t \\right)})⟩ \\\\ &=6 t^{2}\\,i+\\frac{2}{\\left(t - 1\\right)^{2}}\\,j- 4 \\sin{\\left(4 t \\right)}\\,k \\\\ g'(t)&=⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4 t - 2),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(e^{4 t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\cos{\\left(4 t \\right)})⟩ \\\\ &=4\\,i+4 e^{4 t}\\,j- 4 \\sin{\\left(4 t \\right)}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2f(t)+2g(t))'&=2f'(\\!t)+2g'(\\!t) \\\\ &=2\\left(6 t^{2}\\,i+\\frac{2}{\\left(t - 1\\right)^{2}}\\,j- 4 \\sin{\\left(4 t \\right)}\\,k\\right)+2\\left(4\\,i+4 e^{4 t}\\,j- 4 \\sin{\\left(4 t \\right)}\\,k\\right) \\\\ &=\\left(12 t^{2}\\,i+\\frac{4}{\\left(t - 1\\right)^{2}}\\,j- 8 \\sin{\\left(4 t \\right)}\\,k\\right)+\\left(8\\,i+8 e^{4 t}\\,j- 8 \\sin{\\left(4 t \\right)}\\,k\\right) \\\\ &=(12 t^{2} + 8)\\,i+(8 e^{4 t} + \\frac{4}{\\left(t - 1\\right)^{2}})\\,j- 16 \\sin{\\left(4 t \\right)}\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532888 :question/problem "Find the derivative of $-2f(t)+2g(t)$ with respect to $t$, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t)&=(3 t^{3} + 4)\\,i- \\frac{3}{t + 3}\\,j+\\cos{\\left(4 t \\right)}\\,k \\\\ g(t)&=(4 t - 3)\\,i+e^{2 t}\\,j+\\cos{\\left(4 t \\right)}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the constant multiple rule and the sum rule, we have\n\n$$\n(-2f(t)+2g(t))'=-2f'(\\!t)+2g'(\\!t).\n$$\n\nLet's find the derivatives of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f'(t)&=⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3 t^{3} + 4),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(- \\frac{3}{t + 3}\\right),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\cos{\\left(4 t \\right)})⟩ \\\\ &=9 t^{2}\\,i+\\frac{3}{\\left(t + 3\\right)^{2}}\\,j- 4 \\sin{\\left(4 t \\right)}\\,k \\\\ g'(t)&=⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4 t - 3),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(e^{2 t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\cos{\\left(4 t \\right)})⟩ \\\\ &=4\\,i+2 e^{2 t}\\,j- 4 \\sin{\\left(4 t \\right)}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-2f(t)+2g(t))'&=-2f'(\\!t)+2g'(\\!t) \\\\ &=-2\\left(9 t^{2}\\,i+\\frac{3}{\\left(t + 3\\right)^{2}}\\,j- 4 \\sin{\\left(4 t \\right)}\\,k\\right)+2\\left(4\\,i+2 e^{2 t}\\,j- 4 \\sin{\\left(4 t \\right)}\\,k\\right) \\\\ &=\\left(- 18 t^{2}\\,i- \\frac{6}{\\left(t + 3\\right)^{2}}\\,j+8 \\sin{\\left(4 t \\right)}\\,k\\right)+\\left(8\\,i+4 e^{2 t}\\,j- 8 \\sin{\\left(4 t \\right)}\\,k\\right) \\\\ &=(8 - 18 t^{2})\\,i+(4 e^{2 t} - \\frac{6}{\\left(t + 3\\right)^{2}})\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532891 :question/problem "Find the derivative of $-2f(t)+2g(t)$ with respect to $t$, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t)&=(4 t^{2} + 2)\\,i- \\frac{4}{t + 3}\\,j+\\cos{\\left(3 t \\right)}\\,k \\\\ g(t)&=(2 t - 4)\\,i+e^{2 t}\\,j+\\cos{\\left(3 t \\right)}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the constant multiple rule and the sum rule, we have\n\n$$\n(-2f(t)+2g(t))'=-2f'(\\!t)+2g'(\\!t).\n$$\n\nLet's find the derivatives of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f'(t)&=⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4 t^{2} + 2),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(- \\frac{4}{t + 3}\\right),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\cos{\\left(3 t \\right)})⟩ \\\\ &=8 t\\,i+\\frac{4}{\\left(t + 3\\right)^{2}}\\,j- 3 \\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)}\\,k \\\\ g'(t)&=⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2 t - 4),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(e^{2 t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\cos{\\left(3 t \\right)})⟩ \\\\ &=2\\,i+2 e^{2 t}\\,j- 3 \\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-2f(t)+2g(t))'&=-2f'(\\!t)+2g'(\\!t) \\\\ &=-2\\left(8 t\\,i+\\frac{4}{\\left(t + 3\\right)^{2}}\\,j- 3 \\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)}\\,k\\right)+2\\left(2\\,i+2 e^{2 t}\\,j- 3 \\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)}\\,k\\right) \\\\ &=\\left(- 16 t\\,i- \\frac{8}{\\left(t + 3\\right)^{2}}\\,j+6 \\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)}\\,k\\right)+\\left(4\\,i+4 e^{2 t}\\,j- 6 \\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)}\\,k\\right) \\\\ &=(4 - 16 t)\\,i+(4 e^{2 t} - \\frac{8}{\\left(t + 3\\right)^{2}})\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532929 :question/problem "Find $k$ given that the vectors $a=⟨0,2,2⟩$, $b=⟨-1,3,-1⟩$, and $c=⟨k,0,1⟩$ are coplanar." :question/worked-solution "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot(b\\times c)&=\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -1 \\\\ k & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\\\ &=((0)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix})+((-2)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ k & 1\\end{vmatrix})+((2)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ k & 0\\end{vmatrix})\\\\ &=((0)\\cdot ((3)\\cdot (1)-(-1)\\cdot (0)))+((-2)\\cdot ((-1)\\cdot (1)-(-1)\\cdot (k)))+((2)\\cdot ((-1)\\cdot (0)-(3)\\cdot (k)))\\\\ &=((0)\\cdot (3))+((-2)\\cdot (k-1))+((2)\\cdot (-3k))\\\\ &=(0)+(2-2k)+(-6k)\\\\ &=2-8k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2-8k&=0\\\\ -8k&=-2\\\\ k&=\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=\\frac{1}{4}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532943 :question/problem "Find $k$ given that the vectors $a=⟨-2,-4,-4⟩$, $b=⟨5,k,3⟩$, and $c=⟨-8,-4,-6⟩$ are coplanar.\n\n$k=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot(b\\times c)&=\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & -4 & -4 \\\\ 5 & k & 3 \\\\ -8 & -4 & -6\\end{vmatrix}\\\\ &=((-2)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}k & 3 \\\\ -4 & -6\\end{vmatrix})+((4)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}5 & 3 \\\\ -8 & -6\\end{vmatrix})+((-4)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}5 & k \\\\ -8 & -4\\end{vmatrix})\\\\ &=((-2)\\cdot ((k)\\cdot (-6)-(3)\\cdot (-4)))+((4)\\cdot ((5)\\cdot (-6)-(3)\\cdot (-8)))+((-4)\\cdot ((5)\\cdot (-4)-(k)\\cdot (-8)))\\\\ &=((-2)\\cdot (12-6k))+((4)\\cdot (-6))+((-4)\\cdot (8k-20))\\\\ &=(12k-24)+(-24)+(80-32k)\\\\ &=32-20k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}32-20k&=0\\\\ -20k&=-32\\\\ k&=\\frac{8}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=\\frac{8}{5}.$"}]}
